1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,212
Everything in the last two lectures took real hours: reading transcripts, arguing

2
00:00:05,212 --> 00:00:08,214
about wording, running a calibration loop by hand.

3
00:00:08,214 --> 00:00:09,955
Here's the tempting shortcut.

4
00:00:09,955 --> 00:00:14,877
Hand your rubric and your fifty cases to an AI model, and it scores all of them in

5
00:00:14,877 --> 00:00:16,558
under a minute, for pennies.

6
00:00:16,558 --> 00:00:20,760
That speed is real, and for most teams it's eventually the right call.

7
00:00:20,760 --> 00:00:22,501
But notice what you just did.

8
00:00:22,501 --> 00:00:27,304
You handed judgement to a system built from the same technology you're trying to

9
00:00:27,304 --> 00:00:30,725
evaluate, and asked it to grade its own kind of homework.

10
00:00:30,725 --> 00:00:35,528
Would you trust a student grading their own exam without checking the answer key

11
00:00:35,528 --> 00:00:35,888
first?

12
00:00:35,888 --> 00:00:38,649
That's the position you just put the model in.

13
00:00:38,649 --> 00:00:40,030
It's not disqualifying.

14
00:00:40,030 --> 00:00:42,792
It's a reason to check, not to trust outright.

15
00:00:42,792 --> 00:00:44,833
An LLM judge doesn't lie randomly.

16
00:00:44,833 --> 00:00:49,695
It lies in a small number of specific, well-documented ways, the kind researchers

17
00:00:49,695 --> 00:00:54,437
have measured across thousands of comparisons, and once you know what they are,

18
00:00:54,437 --> 00:00:56,418
they're straightforward to catch.

19
00:00:56,418 --> 00:01:01,161
This isn't a reason to skip straight back to scoring everything by hand either.

20
00:01:01,161 --> 00:01:03,682
Section 4 has already cost you real hours.

21
00:01:03,682 --> 00:01:08,605
The judge is how those hours stop scaling linearly with how much traffic you have.

22
00:01:09,155 --> 00:01:12,667
Two are worth knowing before you use this on anything real.

23
00:01:12,667 --> 00:01:17,429
Length bias: judge models consistently rate longer answers higher, even when the

24
00:01:17,429 --> 00:01:18,858
extra words add nothing.

25
00:01:18,858 --> 00:01:23,680
Politeness, hedging, restating the question, all of it reads as thoroughness to a

26
00:01:23,680 --> 00:01:27,669
model pattern-matching on what confident answers usually look like.

27
00:01:27,669 --> 00:01:32,431
Position bias shows up when you ask a judge to compare two outputs side by side.

28
00:01:32,431 --> 00:01:37,193
Swap which one appears first, and on a meaningful fraction of cases, the verdict

29
00:01:37,193 --> 00:01:38,027
flips with it.

30
00:01:38,027 --> 00:01:40,527
The judge isn't re-weighing the arguments.

31
00:01:40,527 --> 00:01:44,039
It's showing a mild preference for whichever it read first.

32
00:01:44,039 --> 00:01:46,421
Neither is a reason to abandon the idea.

33
00:01:46,421 --> 00:01:51,302
Both have a five-minute check: does the score track word count more than it tracks

34
00:01:51,302 --> 00:01:56,065
the rubric, and does the verdict survive being run again with the order swapped.

35
00:01:56,065 --> 00:02:01,065
A third is worth a mention even though it's harder to check yourself: judges tend to

36
00:02:01,065 --> 00:02:06,066
favour answers formatted with headings and bullet points over plain prose making the

37
00:02:06,066 --> 00:02:07,197
identical argument.

38
00:02:07,197 --> 00:02:11,959
If your rubric never mentioned formatting, and the judge is rewarding it anyway,

39
00:02:11,959 --> 00:02:15,233
that preference is coming from the judge, not from you.

40
00:02:15,783 --> 00:02:20,521
Here's the bias caught in the act, on the ticket-summary case you already know.

41
00:02:20,521 --> 00:02:25,139
Two summaries, same underlying facts: charge, refund, deadline, likely cause.

42
00:02:25,139 --> 00:02:27,898
One is forty-one words, direct, nothing extra.

43
00:02:27,898 --> 00:02:32,636
The other is a hundred and eighteen words, the same four facts, restated with a

44
00:02:32,636 --> 00:02:34,675
longer opening and a longer close.

45
00:02:34,675 --> 00:02:38,334
Scored against your six-question rubric by a human, they tie.

46
00:02:38,334 --> 00:02:41,452
Every fact that needs to survive, survives, in both.

47
00:02:41,452 --> 00:02:46,490
Scored by an unchecked LLM judge, the padded version wins outright: five out of five

48
00:02:46,490 --> 00:02:48,049
against three out of five.

49
00:02:48,049 --> 00:02:50,988
Nothing about the padded version is more correct.

50
00:02:50,988 --> 00:02:55,666
It just sounds more thorough, and "sounds thorough" is precisely the pattern a

51
00:02:55,666 --> 00:02:57,345
length-biased judge rewards.

52
00:02:57,345 --> 00:03:02,023
Ship on that judge's numbers alone and you'd be optimising your feature to get

53
00:03:02,023 --> 00:03:03,223
wordier, not better.

54
00:03:03,773 --> 00:03:05,760
You already own the tool for this.

55
00:03:05,760 --> 00:03:10,552
It's the calibration loop from last lecture, run once more with a different second

56
00:03:10,552 --> 00:03:11,078
reviewer.

57
00:03:11,078 --> 00:03:15,462
Score fifteen real cases yourself, using the rubric you already calibrated.

58
00:03:15,462 --> 00:03:19,436
Have the model score the same fifteen, without seeing your verdicts.

59
00:03:19,436 --> 00:03:23,644
Compare, case by case, the same way you compared against your colleague.

60
00:03:23,644 --> 00:03:28,437
Clear roughly eighty-five percent agreement, and you've earned the right to let it

61
00:03:28,437 --> 00:03:32,236
score the other thirty-five, and every future release after that.

62
00:03:32,236 --> 00:03:37,087
Fall short, and look at where it broke: if the disagreements cluster on your longer

63
00:03:37,087 --> 00:03:40,243
outputs, you've found length bias, not a broken judge.

64
00:03:40,243 --> 00:03:43,575
This is also the one honest way to know a judge is ready.

65
00:03:43,575 --> 00:03:45,036
Not a vendor's benchmark.

66
00:03:45,036 --> 00:03:47,841
Your rubric, on your cases, checked against you.

67
00:03:48,391 --> 00:03:49,709
Say the cost plainly.

68
00:03:49,709 --> 00:03:53,848
Per case, an LLM judge is close to free next to a human afternoon.

69
00:03:53,848 --> 00:03:57,486
But "validated once" is not the same claim as "validated".

70
00:03:57,486 --> 00:04:02,755
The model behind your judge gets updated on a schedule you don't control and usually

71
00:04:02,755 --> 00:04:07,898
aren't told about, and an update can shift how it scores without changing a single

72
00:04:07,898 --> 00:04:09,153
word of your rubric.

73
00:04:09,153 --> 00:04:13,982
That's the same drift idea from earlier in this section, aimed at a different

74
00:04:13,982 --> 00:04:14,421
target.

75
00:04:14,421 --> 00:04:18,122
There, production traffic moved and your golden set didn't.

76
00:04:18,122 --> 00:04:21,697
Here, the judge itself moves and your validation doesn't.

77
00:04:21,697 --> 00:04:26,903
The fix costs almost nothing: rerun the fifteen-case check against your judge every

78
00:04:26,903 --> 00:04:31,858
time you notice its provider ships an update, or on a calendar, whichever comes

79
00:04:31,858 --> 00:04:32,234
first.

80
00:04:32,234 --> 00:04:36,186
Treat "validated" as a date-stamped claim, not a permanent one.

81
00:04:36,186 --> 00:04:39,510
Put the date on the number itself when you report it.

82
00:04:39,510 --> 00:04:44,340
"Ninety-four percent, validated against a human rubric on the third of March"

83
00:04:44,340 --> 00:04:47,162
survives a hard question in a review meeting.

84
00:04:47,162 --> 00:04:49,734
A bare "ninety-four percent" invites one.

85
00:04:50,284 --> 00:04:52,971
Notice the word doing the work again: recently.

86
00:04:52,971 --> 00:04:54,514
Not "we checked this once".

87
00:04:54,514 --> 00:04:56,858
Trustworthy is a claim with a shelf life.

88
00:04:56,858 --> 00:05:00,803
The exercise reuses cases you've already scored, which is deliberate.

89
00:05:00,803 --> 00:05:05,376
You're not starting a new curation project, you're pointing a second reviewer at

90
00:05:05,376 --> 00:05:06,805
work you've already done.

91
00:05:06,805 --> 00:05:11,092
One afternoon, fifteen cases, a model, and a rubric it's never seen before.

92
00:05:11,092 --> 00:05:15,266
Sort the disagreements by word count first, before you read a single one.

93
00:05:15,266 --> 00:05:20,068
If they cluster at the long end, you've found your bias before it found you, and you

94
00:05:20,068 --> 00:05:22,411
know exactly which slide to come back to.

95
00:05:22,411 --> 00:05:26,356
If they don't cluster anywhere obvious, that's a genuinely good sign.

96
00:05:26,356 --> 00:05:30,986
It means the disagreements are ordinary judgement calls rather than a pattern you

97
00:05:30,986 --> 00:05:35,388
can name, which is exactly what you'd expect from two competent reviewers who

98
00:05:35,388 --> 00:05:37,446
occasionally see things differently.

99
00:05:37,996 --> 00:05:39,761
One thing before you move on.

100
00:05:39,761 --> 00:05:44,812
Take the fifteen cases from last lecture's calibration and add one more reviewer: a

101
00:05:44,812 --> 00:05:47,612
model, scoring blind, against the same rubric.

102
00:05:47,612 --> 00:05:50,168
You already have everything else you need.

103
00:05:50,168 --> 00:05:55,281
If the agreement isn't there yet, sort the disagreements by word count before you do

104
00:05:55,281 --> 00:05:56,376
anything cleverer.

105
00:05:56,376 --> 00:06:01,428
It's the single most common cause, and checking it first will save you an afternoon

106
00:06:01,428 --> 00:06:03,436
of chasing the wrong explanation.

107
00:06:03,436 --> 00:06:08,487
Next lecture, we stop scoring one batch of fifty and start watching the number over

108
00:06:08,487 --> 00:06:13,478
time: pass rate, regression testing, and the dashboard that tells you the moment a

109
00:06:13,478 --> 00:06:16,582
change makes things worse, instead of a week later.
